ZTEXT001000010f9ded1f6d40000000
Kernel audit for Zero OS 0.2.2

Scope and implementation
Only kernel.zero was changed. All implementation remains in Zero. The
ZeroFS layout, checksums, generation ordering, command names and explicit
formatting policy are unchanged. Existing data.img was not modified.

Fixed: commands with no arguments silently accepted extra input.
help, info, clear and watch now report an argument error before acting.

Fixed: structurally invalid snapshots could contain duplicate used names.
Lookup previously selected the last matching record, and deleting it could
expose a different record under the same name. Validation now compares each
used name with earlier used records and rejects the entire duplicate bank.
The existing highest-valid-generation selection then permits recovery from
the other bank without writing to disk.

Fixed: ATA transfers assumed a compatible 512-byte disk before identification.
Startup now sends read-only IDENTIFY DEVICE and requires an ATA disk, LBA28,
at least 18 sectors, 512-byte logical sectors, and valid FLUSH CACHE support.
All sector reads, writes and flushes are gated on this result. Incompatible
or absent disks leave the terminal usable and format ZERO reports that
nothing changed. A valid word 106 advertising larger logical sectors is
rejected; older devices without that field retain ATA's 512-byte default.

Fixed: the driver issued commands when DRQ could still be asserted, and
immediately failed a completed transfer if DRQ took time to clear. Idle and
post-transfer waits now poll until both BSY and DRQ clear, with the existing
bounded timeout. Post-command ERR/DF still terminate the request as errors.

Clarified: format's token comparison now compares bytes through the NUL.
The former load32 did not actually permit short tokens to match ZERO:
their NUL necessarily differed from the required byte, and the input buffer
had sufficient space. This is clarity/hardening, not a demonstrated erasure
bug. The exact token and no-extra-arguments requirement are retained.

Validation performed
./build/zero --raw < kernel.zero > build/kernel-audit.bin succeeded.
The resulting candidate kernel is 22511 bytes. It was booted with the
existing Zero loader/image builder in QEMU 10.0.13, OVMF, pc/TCG, 128 MiB,
using separate test disks. build/kernel.bin and build/system.img were not
overwritten by this audit.

Passed in the candidate VM:
- Mount of the existing filesystem format on a copied data disk.
- create/save/read/watch and create preserving existing nonempty content.
- Separate QEMU process restart preserving saved text.
- help/info/clear/watch reject extra arguments; write still prints text.
- format Z, format ZER and format ZERO extra preserve the existing file.
- Explicit format ZERO empties the filesystem; create/read/rm then work.
- A 17-sector disk stays unavailable. format ZERO is refused, the terminal
  still accepts write, and the test disk's SHA-256 is unchanged.
- No primary-master disk: unavailable filesystem, refused format, usable
  terminal. The boot medium remained a separate primary-slave disk.
- Primary-master ATAPI CD device: unavailable filesystem, refused format,
  usable terminal; the medium's SHA-256 is unchanged.
- Duplicate-name fixture: bank A generation 3 held a=one and b=two. Changed
  the second name b to a at byte 1025, then subtracted 1026 from both the
  data checksum at byte 16 and header sum at byte 24. Independently
  recomputed the weighted checksum: 700273, equal to the stored checksum.
  Bank B generation 2 held only a=one. Boot selected bank B: watch printed
  a once, read a printed one, and read b reported File not found. Thus the
  newer bank was rejected for structure despite valid checksums.

Compact captured serial logs are in verification/kernel-audit-0.2.2.ztext.
Fixture images and firmware variable copies were temporary audit artifacts.

Remaining targeted validation
Physical hardware, large-logical-sector ATA devices, devices lacking FLUSH
CACHE, and artificial delayed-DRQ/error injection are not tested. QEMU's IDE
device rejected a requested 4096-byte logical size because its discard
granularity restrictions require 512 bytes; that case was not boot-tested.
The code paths were independently reviewed. A future device-level harness
could exercise these responses without expanding OS language dependencies.
